      body:
        "**Why a son mattered so much**\n\nBy the mid-1520s Henry had been married to Catherine for over 15 years. She had been pregnant at least six times, but only one child survived: **Princess Mary** (born 1516). A son, Prince Henry, born on New Year's Day 1511, lived for only about seven weeks. Catherine was now around 40 and unlikely to have more children.\n\nTo Henry this was a political crisis, not just a private sadness:\n\n- Only one woman, **Matilda** in the 1100s, had tried to rule England in her own right — and the result was civil war.\n- Many people still remembered the **Wars of the Roses**. Without a clear male heir, rivals might fight again.\n- A queen would probably marry a foreign prince, who might take control of England.\n\n**Anne Boleyn**\n\nBy about 1526 Henry had fallen in love with **Anne Boleyn**, a lady-in-waiting educated at the French court. Anne refused to become simply his mistress. Marrying her could give Henry both love and — he hoped — a legitimate son.\n\n**Henry's argument: Leviticus**\n\nHenry claimed his marriage had never been valid. The Bible says (Leviticus 20:21) that a man who marries his brother's wife will be **childless**; Henry argued that his lack of a son was God's punishment. Catherine replied that her marriage to Arthur had never been a full marriage, and that the Pope had given permission in 1503–04 anyway. Another passage (Deuteronomy 25:5) seemed to say the opposite of Leviticus.\n\n**Why the Pope would not agree**\n\nIn **May 1527** the troops of **Charles V** — Holy Roman Emperor, King of Spain and **Catherine's nephew** — sacked Rome. **Pope Clement VII** was effectively in Charles's power. Granting an annulment would insult Charles and admit that an earlier Pope had been wrong.\n\n**The fall of Wolsey**\n\nWolsey was ordered to get the annulment. In 1529 a special church court met at **Blackfriars** in London with the Pope's representative, Cardinal Campeggio. Catherine knelt before Henry, appealed to him in person, then walked out. In July Campeggio adjourned the court and the case was moved to Rome. Wolsey had failed. In **1529** he lost his post as Lord Chancellor; arrested for treason in 1530, he died on the journey to London.\n\n| Year | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 1516 | Mary born |\n| c.1526 | Henry pursues Anne Boleyn |\n| 1527 | Henry seeks an annulment; Sack of Rome |\n| 1529 | Blackfriars court fails; Wolsey falls |",
