      whyItWorks:
        "Why did a dispute about one marriage turn into a revolution in the Church? Because once Henry denied the Pope's authority over his marriage, he had to deny it **everywhere** — otherwise the Pope could still overrule him. Cromwell's genius was to make the break **legal**: each step was an Act of Parliament, so resisting it meant breaking the law of England, not just disagreeing with a king. That is why the Treason Act followed so quickly. The result was the biggest increase in royal power since 1066 — the king now controlled the Church that had limited every English king before him, including William I and John.",
